# Citation discipline — no unsourced constants, ever

Every magic number — register, baud, timeout, RX window, sleep current — is a claim about
physical reality. An unsourced claim is a guess, and guesses brick nodes nobody can reach.
`scripts/check_citations.py` enforces the format; `docs/CITATIONS.md` is the URL registry.

```c
// CITE(datasheet): RAK5802 RS-485 IO module pinout — https://docs.rakwireless.com/...
static const uint32_t MODBUS_FRAME_GAP_US = 4010; // 3.5 chars @ 4800 8N1
```

In markdown: `[CITE(datasheet): RAK4631 datasheet](https://...)`. Categories: `datasheet`
(vendor primary doc), `spec` (normative standard), `policy` (network operator rule),
`prior-art` (another implementation), `sibling` (local companion repo — cite path **and**
commit SHA), `bench` (our own measurement in `docs/EVIDENCE.md`).

## Minimum bar per change

| Change type | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Any firmware behavior change | ≥ 3 citations across ≥ 2 categories |
| Any register, address, baud, slave ID, channel/type ID | `datasheet` or `spec` on the same or adjacent line |
| Any timing, timeout, retry, backoff, interval | `spec` or `policy`, plus `prior-art` where one exists |
| Any power/sleep/current claim | `datasheet` for the figure, `bench` once measured |
| Payload encode/decode | `spec` (Cayenne LPP) and `sibling` (the live TTN decoder) |
| Docs describing behavior | Same bar as the code |
| Pure refactor, typo, formatting | None new; must not delete existing ones |

**Prior art is not authority** — it shows something works, not that it is correct. Never let it
stand alone behind a register map, timing value, or RF parameter; when it disagrees with the
datasheet, the datasheet wins and the disagreement gets written down.

**Verify, don't recall.** Fetch the URL before citing it — five from-memory URLs were 404s
during setup (corrections log at the end of `docs/CITATIONS.md`). Never commit `UNVERIFIED`;
cite the specific section, not a doc hub; record what the source establishes in your own words.
When sources conflict, do not silently pick one: write an ADR in `docs/decisions/` with both
citations (ADR-0002 holds two known live conflicts — read it before touching the battery
current sign or the payload channel map).
